Subject: [PATCH] fix(test): TEST-01 - three suites that were failing before
 this series, one of which never ran at all
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`pnpm test:web` reported 3 failed files and 2 failed tests. None of them is
caused by TRUTH-01 or GRAIN-01, and I checked that before touching anything
rather than assuming it: neither commit goes near any of the three files, and
`git log` puts the last edit to each of them well before this series.

  lib/floor-assembly.test.ts     never passed since VIS-05 introduced it
  tests/drift.test.ts            over budget since MACH-01 (f0584c1)
  tests/floor-studio.test.ts     broken by LIVE-01 (7678b10)

The common cause is that `pnpm test:web` had not been run. Every one of these
was reachable at any point by running it.

1. lib/floor-assembly.test.ts — A SUITE THAT HAS NEVER EXECUTED ONE ASSERTION

tsconfig.json says `"jsx": "preserve"`, which is right, because Next owns that
transform. esbuild reads it, finds no runtime it can emit, and falls back to
the CLASSIC one — `React.createElement`. A Next component has no reason to
import the React default, so FloorAssembly does not, and rendering it threw
`ReferenceError: React is not defined` at its opening `return (`.

That throw happens inside a describe callback, at COLLECTION time, so it did
not fail one test — it killed the file. Vitest printed `(0)`. Roughly sixty
assertions have never run, among them the one written specifically to catch
F-205: `26026+ Years in Toronto`, three copies of one number concatenating in
the served HTML, which no source-level grep can see. The suite that exists to
catch a duplication bug was itself silently absent.

`esbuild: { jsx: 'automatic' }` in apps/web/vitest.config.ts. This is the only
suite that renders a component; the rest import data out of .tsx modules
without executing JSX, which is why nothing else noticed.

WHAT I COULD NOT VERIFY, AND WHY. This sandbox cannot install from npm (403),
so there is no react to render with and I could not run this suite. I am not
claiming it goes green. What I did instead was bound the risk, because these
assertions were written at VIS-07 against a rendered output that may have
moved since:

  · DESIGN-01 is the only commit to touch FloorAssembly.tsx after the test was
    written, and its entire diff to that file is one COMMENT line — "five
    species" to "six species". The rendered HTML is unchanged.
  · ASSEMBLY_SPECIES is `= FLOOR_PRODUCTS` and ASSEMBLY_WIDTHS `= BOARD_WIDTHS`,
    so DESIGN-03's sixth species cannot desynchronise them. Checked by running
    both comparisons.
  · The one assertion GRAIN-01 could plausibly reach — `urls.size === 1` over
    `/textures/...` in the markup — is safe, because GRAIN-01 changed tile
    CONTENT and kept every filename.

If it surfaces real failures, they are real and they have been real for weeks;
send me the output.

2. tests/drift.test.ts — THE CORE OF llms.txt WAS 33,066 AGAINST A 30,000 CAP

MACH-01 took app/llms.txt/route.ts from 28,237 bytes to 33,019 and pushed the
curated core past its budget. The comment on that budget says what to do, in as
many words: if the core ever crosses, prune the core rather than move the
number. So the number did not move.

Measured, section by section:

  Preferred citation targets  5533   0 links
  Who this is                 5019   0 links
  FAQ                         4773   0 links
  Evidence                    4012  18 links
  Service areas               2478   1 link
  ...

The FAQ is the only one of the three large sections that is neither the entity
nor a citation target — the two things that comment names the core as being
for — and it carried not one link. An agent reading the top of this file to
learn who to cite skipped all 4,773 bytes of it.

It is MOVED, not dropped: it now sits two lines under the sentence in the
Optional preamble that already promises exactly this, that everything below is
also at /llms-full.txt and /api/knowledge, and it is still in the FAQPage
JSON-LD on the page. Core 33,066 → 28,293. The whole file is byte-identical at
55,975, which is the check that it was moved rather than trimmed.

3. tests/floor-studio.test.ts — A CLAIM BROKEN BY A LINE BREAK

floorStudioToMarkdown builds a hard-wrapped blockquote out of an array of
lines, and the wrap fell between "never" and "uploaded":

    '> in the browser and is never',
    '> uploaded, and no image model is involved: ...',

So the file said "never\n> uploaded" and `/never uploaded/` could not match.
Invisible in the rendered markdown, fatal to the assertion that this file
states the privacy claim at all. The test was right and the source was wrong:
a claim a machine is meant to quote should not be breakable by a line break.
Rewrapped so the phrase is contiguous, with a note saying why it must stay so.

NO TEST WAS WEAKENED. No assertion was relaxed, no threshold moved, no suite
skipped. Two are source fixes and one makes a suite run that was not running.

VERIFIED

  65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css fails only here, for want of node_modules,
  and passed in the Codespace on the previous run.
  llms.txt run in-process: core 28,293 < 30,000, body 55,975 < 64,000, 56 links
  in 20..60, every link on the canonical host.
  floorStudioToMarkdown: heading correct, /never uploaded/ matches, all six
  species and all four board widths present.
  ASSEMBLY_SPECIES and ASSEMBLY_WIDTHS compared element-wise against the
  catalogue.
  Suite 1 could not be executed here. Stated above rather than implied.

